[interviewer · EXHIBIT · 1:01] Understood. Given the evidence so far, what additional actions could Brew & Bean take to improve profitability beyond changing menu prices?

[candidate · 1:07] Beyond price, I'd go after the input basket itself, since that's where the roughly 40 points of unfunded cost growth sits.

First, procurement. Beans are up 40% and the dairy/packaging/food basket 37.5%. With 200 locations they have real scale — I'd consolidate vendors, move to annual or multi-year contracts, and hedge green coffee forward rather than buying spot. Packaging in particular should be commoditized and competitively bid.

Second, mix. Shift promotion toward higher-margin items — brewed coffee and espresso drinks over dairy-heavy and food items — which improves blended COGS without touching a single posted price.

Third, waste and portioning. In specialty coffee, dairy spoilage and over-pour are a known leak. Standardized portioning and tighter par levels can take a few points off COGS.

Fourth, overhead — it's only 2 points, but it's the one line that's drifting with no volume justification.

Do we have gross margin by menu item? That would let me size the mix lever properly.
