[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 1:25] Understood. What is the biggest risk to your recommendation, and how would you mitigate it?

[candidate · 1:32] The biggest risk is demand elasticity. Specialty coffee is discretionary and heavily substitutable — a customer can trade down to drip, go to a competitor, or just brew at home. If I push price to recover an input basket up 38% and volume falls more than a few points, I've traded margin recovery for traffic decline and ended up flat or worse. And because the hole is so large — roughly 40 points of unfunded cost growth — the price move required is not small, which makes the elasticity risk sharper than in a typical pass-through situation.

Mitigation: don't do it all at once. Test in two or three geographically distinct regions, hold control markets, and measure transaction counts and average ticket weekly, not just revenue. Stage increases over two or three quarters. Concentrate increases on lower-visibility and habitual items where customers anchor less on price, and hold a visible value item flat.

And run procurement in parallel so pricing doesn't have to carry the whole load.
