[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 1:43] Understood. If the test shows a broad transaction decline, what evidence would you need before deciding to cap price increases and shift the remaining effort to costs?

[candidate silent 6s]

[candidate · 1:50] I'd want three things before capping.

One, a clean elasticity estimate. Transactions, average ticket, and gross profit per store per week in test versus control, run long enough — eight to twelve weeks — to get past the initial sticker-shock period. Customers often return after a few weeks. Capping on four weeks of data would be premature.

Two, confirmation it's price and not something else. Did a competitor promote into those markets? Was there weather, a local closure, a staffing issue? I'd want the control markets to rule that out.

Three, the breakeven. At what price point does gross profit per store peak? If profit still rises at a 6% increase and only turns negative at 10%, we cap at 6 rather than zero.

Only once I see gross profit per store genuinely declining across controlled markets would I cap. And I'd be clear that capping leaves most of the 40-point gap unaddressed, which forces the harder cost conversations.
